Background

Processes can execute concurrently. They may be interrupted at any time, partially completing execution. Concurrent access to shared data may result in data inconsistency. Maintaining data consistency requires mechanisms to ensure the orderly execution of cooperating processes.

Producer-Consumer Problem

This is a paradigm for cooperating processes, where a *producer* process produces information that is consumed by a *consumer* process. An unbounded-buffer places no practical limit on the size of the buffer, while a *bounded-buffer* assumes that there is a fixed buffer size.

